\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The Wilson symbol}
\date{2026-11-23}

\p{Take the [Wilson operator](fdip-009U) at trivial links,
with spacing #{a>0} and #{c_W>0}. Its coefficients are
##{f_j(r)=a^{-1}\sin p_j,\qquad
m(r)=c_Wa^{-2}\sum_j(2-2\cos p_j).}
Each summand in the mass is nonnegative and vanishes only at
#{p_j=0} modulo #{2\pi}. Hence only the origin gives a zero
block, as in \ref{fdip-009V}. Its local degree is #{1} by
\ref{fdip-0142}, since the positive rescaling preserves signs.
With a site-independent self-adjoint grading #{\Gamma} on
#{S\otimes F} anticommuting with every #{\varepsilon_j^S\otimes\bbI_F},
the nonzero mass at other momenta breaks exact anticommutation
by \ref{fdip-0143}.}

\p{If #{e\ge1} periods are even, the #{2^e-1} other vertex
zeros of #{f} have nonzero mass and total local degree #{-1}.
Their degrees are the products #{\prod_j\cos p_j} from
\ref{fdip-0142}. If every period is odd, no other zero of #{f}
is a vertex, and the total degree between vertices is #{-1}.
In the constant kernel the first generator exchanges the two
eigenspaces of any such #{\Gamma}, so its graded index is zero
in every dimension. The volume grading #{C} realizes this in
even dimension (\ref{fdip-009X}). The sign #{+1} counts the
orientation of one momentum zero.}
